To retry a batch without writing it twice, tag the append with an application ID and a version. DuckDB commits the append only if the table's last recorded version for that ID is the one you expect. This uses Delta's per-application transaction versions.

Within a transaction, tag the append with `delta_set_transaction_version(⟨table⟩, ⟨app_id⟩, ⟨new_version⟩, ⟨expected_previous_version⟩)`:

```sql
ATTACH '⟨path⟩' AS my_table (TYPE delta);

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
CALL delta_set_transaction_version('my_table', 'my_app_id', 1::UBIGINT, NULL::UBIGINT);
INSERT INTO my_table VALUES (1);
COMMIT;
```

If another process committed a different version for `my_app_id` first, `COMMIT` fails. A rolled-back transaction leaves the version unchanged. Read the current version with `delta_get_transaction_version(⟨table⟩, ⟨app_id⟩)`, which returns `NULL` if no version has been recorded yet.

